    level: 3,
    type: "qa",
    q: "What is a gross-up provision, and why do landlords put one in their leases?",
    a: "A gross-up restates variable operating expenses, the ones that rise and fall with occupancy, to what they'd be at a set occupancy, often 95% or 100%. Without it, a landlord in a partly empty building under-recovers: tenants' pro-rata shares cover only part of the variable costs that occupied space generates, and the landlord absorbs the rest.",
    why: "Fixed costs like taxes and insurance don't depend on occupancy; cleaning, utilities and management fees do. When shares are based on the whole building's area, the occupied tenants together own only part of the building, so they reimburse only part of the variable costs, even though they generated nearly all of them. Grossing up fixes that. In base-year leases, grossing up both the base year and later years keeps occupancy swings from creating or erasing recoveries: a lease-up doesn't bill existing tenants for new tenants' cleaning, and falling occupancy doesn't hide real cost increases.",
    formula: "Grossed-up variable costs = actual variable costs × gross-up occupancy ÷ actual occupancy\nFixed costs such as taxes and insurance aren't grossed up",
    example: "A [[100,000]] SF building is [[60%]] leased. Cleaning and utilities cost [[$5.00]] per occupied SF: $300K. A [[10,000]] SF tenant's 10% share is $30K, though servicing its own space costs $50K. Grossed up to [[95%]], the pool is $475K and the tenant pays $47.5K.",
    trap: "Check what gets grossed up and whether it applies to both the base year and later years. Grossing up fixed costs, or only the current year, pushes the cost of vacant space onto tenants, and tenant audits look for exactly that."
