    q: "A shopping center's leases are all net, yet tenants reimburse only 80% of its recoverable expenses. Why?",
    a: "Because net leases rarely pass through everything. The landlord pays the share of vacant space. Anchors often pay a capped or fixed amount, and some tenants have caps or exclusions. The recovery ratio, recoveries divided by recoverable expenses, measures how much of that expense risk the owner still carries.",
    why: "A net label describes the typical lease, not the whole building. Vacancy is the biggest leak: until space is leased, its share of taxes and CAM is the landlord's cost, so leasing up raises recoveries as well as rent. Anchors negotiate their own terms, and the gap they leave falls on the landlord unless shop leases shift it to the shops. Caps and exclusions leak more. So underwrite recoveries lease by lease, then check the ratio against the property's history.",
    formula: "Recovery ratio = recoveries ÷ recoverable operating expenses",
    example: "Recoverable expenses are [[$2.0M]]. With the center [[95%]] leased, the vacant share of $100K isn't recovered. Anchors on [[30%]] of the area pay a fixed [[$350K]] instead of their $600K share, a $250K gap, and CAM caps cost [[$50K]]. Recoveries: $1.6M, an 80% ratio.",
    trap: "Many retail leases add an administrative fee on CAM, often around 10–15%, which can push CAM recoveries above 100% of the cost. Check the leases before counting on it.",
    visual: { kind: "waterfall", unit: "$K", start: { label: "Recoverable expenses", value: 2000 }, steps: [
      { label: "Vacant space", delta: -100 },
      { label: "Anchor gap", delta: -250 },
      { label: "Caps", delta: -50 }
    ], end: { label: "Recoveries", value: 1600 } }
