    id: "re-leases-021",
    track: "re",
    module: "re-leases",
    topic: "Estoppel certificates",
    level: 1,
    type: "qa",
    q: "What is a tenant estoppel certificate, and what does it confirm?",
    a: "It's a signed statement from a tenant confirming the key facts of its lease: that the lease is in force with no defaults or disputes, which documents make it up, the rent and how far it's been paid, the expiry date, options and the security deposit. Buyers and lenders ask for one from each major tenant before closing.",
    why: "The name comes from the legal idea of estoppel: once the tenant certifies these facts, it generally can't later claim otherwise against the party that relied on them. That's what makes a rent roll dependable. A seller's summary can be wrong or incomplete, while a tenant's signature surfaces side agreements, disputed charges, prepaid rent and claims against the landlord. Most commercial leases require tenants to deliver an estoppel within a set number of days after a request, often 10 to 30.",
    trap: "An estoppel confirms facts, not credit. A clean estoppel from a tenant about to fail doesn't make its rent any more collectible."
