    topic: "WALT",
    level: 1,
    type: "qa",
    classic: true,
    q: "What is WALT, and what doesn't it tell you?",
    a: "WALT, the weighted average lease term, is the average time left on a building's leases, weighted by each lease's share of rent or sometimes area. A longer WALT means income is locked in longer. But it's an average: it doesn't show when individual leases expire, whether tenants will renew, how strong they are or whether rents are above market.",
    why: "Weighting by rent measures how long the income is secured, which is what matters for valuation and lending; weighting by area measures how long the space is committed. The average hides the shape: a 5-year WALT could be every lease expiring in year 5, or half the rent expiring next year and half in ten years. So read it with the rollover schedule and compare it with your hold period and the loan's term.",
    formula: "WALT = Σ (annual rent × years remaining) ÷ total annual rent",
    example: "Tenant A pays [[$2.0M]] with [[8]] years left; B pays [[$1.0M]] with [[1]] year left. WALT by rent: (2.0 × 8 + 1.0 × 1) ÷ 3.0 = 5.7 years, yet a third of the rent expires next year.",
    trap: "Say which weighting you use. A single long lease at a high rent can stretch a rent-weighted WALT even while most of the space rolls soon."
