    q: "What is a lease rollover schedule, and what do you look for in one?",
    a: "It shows how much of a building's rent or area expires each year. You look for concentration: a year when a large share rolls, especially if it coincides with your loan maturity or planned sale. Then check who's rolling, whether their rents are above or below market and what re-leasing would cost.",
    why: "Rollover is when income is at risk and capital gets spent. A year with 40% of rent expiring can mean downtime, free rent, TI and commissions on a large block of space at once, wiping out cash flow and complicating a refinancing or sale. Buyers want staggered expiries, and lenders respond to heavy rollover with leasing reserves or cash sweeps. A below-market lease rolling is upside; an above-market one is risk. The schedule also shows where to focus: renewing the biggest tenants early cuts the largest risks.",
