    level: 1,
    type: "qa",
    classic: true,
    q: "What is loss-to-lease, and what can make it hard to capture?",
    a: "Loss-to-lease is the gap between market rent and the rent tenants pay under their leases, a term used mostly for apartments. It's shown as a deduction from gross potential rent at market. It signals upside as leases renew or turn over, but renewal pushback, rent regulation and overstated market rents can keep much of it from ever being collected.",
    why: "Start with the market rent column: it's often an asking rent, not what recent leases achieved, so check signed leases. Owners also run some loss-to-lease on purpose, pricing renewals below new leases because turnover costs more than the discount. Rent caps or stabilization rules can limit increases, and pushing every renewal to market raises move-outs. So buyers underwrite a gradual burn-off, not a one-year jump. The reverse, in-place rent above market, is gain-to-lease: income that falls as leases reset.",
    example: "[[200]] units average [[$1,900]] a month. Against an asking rent of [[$2,100]], loss-to-lease looks like $480K a year; against recent signed leases at [[$2,000]], it's $240K. The choice of market rent halves the upside.",
    trap: "Don't double count. A loss-to-lease line belongs only in a build that starts GPR at market rent; if GPR is already at in-place rents, the gap is already out."
