    q: "What are cash and GAAP leasing spreads, and why do REIT investors watch them?",
    a: "A leasing spread is the change in rent on new and renewal leases against the expiring leases on the same space. The cash spread compares the new lease's starting rent with the old lease's final rent; the GAAP spread compares average rent over each term, including escalations and free rent. Spreads show whether in-place rents were below or above market.",
    why: "Spreads are the realized mark-to-market: evidence that the upside a REIT claims in below-market leases is turning into income. Because leases escalate, the old lease's final rent is its highest and the new lease's first rent is its lowest, so with fixed bumps, cash spreads look worse than GAAP spreads. A positive GAAP spread with a negative cash spread often just reflects escalations. Spreads also ignore the capital it took to sign the lease, so read them with TI, commissions and free rent per SF.",
