    a: "A full guarantee makes a parent or owner liable for everything the tenant owes for the term: the strongest protection, if the guarantor is solid. A limited guarantee caps that, say at a year's rent, or burns down over time. A good-guy guarantee, common in New York, only covers rent until the tenant hands back the space.",
    why: "A guarantee is only as good as the guarantor: one from a thinly capitalized affiliate adds little, while one from a strong parent can make a weak tenant financeable. Limited and burn-down guarantees reflect a bargain: the tenant proves itself over time, and the landlord's exposure shrinks as it recovers its TI and commissions through rent. A good-guy guarantee doesn't protect the rent stream at all. It protects the landlord from a tenant that stops paying but won't leave, so the space can be re-let quickly. Value each lease on the credit that actually stands behind it.",
    example: "A lease pays [[$500K]] a year with [[7]] years left when the tenant fails. With a full guarantee from a solvent parent, the landlord can pursue the remaining $3.5M, less what it earns by re-letting. With a guarantee capped at [[12]] months' rent, it recovers at most $500K. With a good-guy guarantee, it gets rent to the surrender date and its space back.",
    trap: "In a tenant bankruptcy, the cap on the landlord's claim applies to the tenant's estate, not generally to a solvent guarantor. That's when a strong guarantee earns its keep."
