    topic: "NNN vs absolute net leases",
    level: 2,
    type: "qa",
    q: "What's the difference between a triple net lease and an absolute net lease?",
    a: "Under a triple net lease the tenant pays its share of taxes, insurance and operating costs, but the landlord often keeps some obligations, most often roof and structure. Under an absolute net lease the tenant pays everything, including roof, structure and capital repairs. The strictest form, a bondable lease, keeps rent due even if the building is destroyed.",
    why: "NNN is used loosely, so read what the tenant actually pays. Roof, structure and parking lot replacement are big, lumpy capital costs; when they stay with the landlord, its cash flow is less than the rent, so you still reserve for them. Under an absolute net lease the landlord's income is essentially the rent itself, which is why these leases, usually long and signed by single tenants, trade like bonds and price off the tenant's credit. The trade-off is control: the tenant runs and maintains the building, and its upkeep shapes what you own at expiry.",
    example: "Two identical single-tenant buildings each pay [[$500K]] a year. One is absolute net. The other is NNN with the landlord responsible for roof and structure, reserved at [[$0.25]]/SF on [[40,000]] SF: $10K a year. Its cash flow is $490K, 2% less, and a roof replacement could cost far more in a single year.",
    trap: "Check who replaces, not just who maintains. Many NNN leases make the tenant maintain the roof but leave replacement to the landlord, and a buyer who misses that underfunds its reserves."
