    topic: "Anchors and shadow anchors",
    level: 2,
    type: "qa",
    q: "How do anchor leases differ from in-line shop leases, and what is a shadow anchor?",
    a: "Anchors lease large boxes for long terms, often 15 years or more plus options, at low rent per SF, often with capped or fixed expense shares. In-line shops pay far more per SF on shorter leases. A shadow anchor is a big store beside the center that draws its shoppers but belongs to someone else and pays no rent.",
    why: "The center runs on a trade: the landlord gives anchors cheap rent and strong rights because their traffic lets small shops pay high rents. So an anchor's value is mostly the shop rent it supports, not its own rent. Anchors often hold rights over the site, such as limits on what the landlord can build, parking ratios and approval of other uses, set in the lease or a reciprocal easement agreement. A shadow anchor brings traffic for free but with no control: if it closes, the center suffers and the landlord can't re-lease the box.",
    example: "A [[100,000]] SF center: a [[50,000]] SF grocer pays [[$12]]/SF, or $600K; [[50,000]] SF of shops pay [[$32]]/SF, or $1.6M. The anchor fills half the space but brings in 27% of the rent.",
    trap: "Ask whether anchors own their stores. An anchor-owned pad appears on the site plan and in the traffic counts, but it isn't part of your income or the lender's collateral."
