    topic: "Security deposits and letters of credit",
    level: 2,
    type: "qa",
    q: "Why do landlords ask for a security deposit or a letter of credit, and why do they often prefer the letter of credit?",
    a: "Both protect the landlord if the tenant defaults, covering unpaid rent, damage or unrecovered TI and commissions. A cash deposit is held by the landlord; a letter of credit is a bank's promise to pay on demand. Landlords prefer letters of credit for large amounts because the bank pays even if the tenant is insolvent.",
    why: "Security is sized to the landlord's exposure: weaker credit, bigger TI packages and above-standard build-outs justify more, and many leases let it burn down as the tenant proves itself. In a tenant bankruptcy, applying a cash deposit generally needs the court's permission, while a letter of credit is the bank's own obligation, so the landlord can usually draw it. Its value depends on the issuing bank and on keeping it in force, so track expiry dates and automatic renewals.",
    trap: "When a building sells, security moves to the buyer: cash deposits are credited at closing, and each letter of credit must be transferred or reissued to the new owner. Miss one and you're unsecured."
