    a: "An assignment transfers the whole lease to a new tenant, who then deals directly with the landlord; the original tenant usually stays liable unless released. A sublease leaves the original lease in place: the tenant becomes landlord to a subtenant for some or all of its space, and still owes the landlord the full rent.",
    why: "The landlord picked the original tenant for its credit and use, so leases usually require the landlord's consent, often not to be unreasonably withheld. Common protections include a recapture right to take the space back instead of consenting, a share of any sublease profit (often 50%) and keeping the original tenant liable. Sublease space also competes with the landlord's own vacant space, often at a discount, so a lot of it in a market is shadow vacancy: space that's leased and paid for but empty or on offer.",
    trap: "Sublease rent below the lease rent is the tenant's loss, not the landlord's, while the lease lasts. The risk shows up at expiry: a tenant won't renew space it's trying to shed."
