    q: "What are radius restrictions and exclusive-use clauses in retail leases, and whom does each protect?",
    a: "A radius restriction bars the tenant from opening another store within a set distance, often a few miles, during the lease. It protects the landlord, whose traffic and percentage rent would suffer if sales moved nearby. An exclusive bars the landlord from leasing to another tenant whose main use competes with the tenant's. It protects the tenant.",
    why: "Both limit competition, but they bind different parties. A radius clause keeps sales at your center, which matters most where percentage rent applies; tenants resist it because it limits expansion. An exclusive is valuable to the tenant and costly to the landlord, since every exclusive narrows who can lease vacant space. Over time a center can build up exclusives that block whole categories, so buyers map every exclusive and use restriction before assuming vacant space can go to anyone.",
    trap: "Exclusives often lapse if the protected tenant stops operating in its space, but only if the lease says so. Check whether a dark tenant still blocks a use."
