    a: "Going dark means a tenant closes its store but keeps paying rent, which it may do unless the lease has an operating covenant. A kick-out lets a party end the lease early, usually the tenant if sales miss a target by a set date. Landlords care because a dark store kills traffic and a kick-out shortens the real term.",
    why: "A dark store still pays rent but stops drawing shoppers, which hurts its neighbors' sales and can trigger their co-tenancy rights. Anchors rarely accept operating covenants, so landlords negotiate a recapture right: if the tenant goes dark, the landlord can end the lease and take back the space. A tenant kick-out shortens the effective term: a 10-year lease with a year-5 sales kick-out is really a 5-year lease with a tenant option to stay. Some leases give the landlord a kick-out instead, to replace a weak performer. Underwrite a kick-out date as a possible expiry and compare trailing sales with the threshold.",
    trap: "Dark doesn't mean vacant on the rent roll: the tenant keeps paying until the lease ends or is recaptured. But many loans treat a dark anchor as a trigger for a cash sweep, even while its rent keeps coming in."
