    a: "A lease abstract is a short summary of a lease's key terms, so you don't have to reread a long document and its amendments. It covers the parties and guarantor, premises and area, key dates, the rent schedule, recovery terms, concessions, options, security and restrictions such as exclusives and co-tenancy. Rent rolls and models are built from abstracts.",
    why: "A lease is scattered across the original document and years of amendments, and the terms that change value are often buried: a termination right, a cap on recoveries, a below-market renewal option, a co-tenancy trigger. Abstracting them in a standard format lets you check the rent roll, model each lease correctly and spot risks across the building. Mistakes compound, because a wrong base year or a missed option flows straight into NOI and value. Abstract from the lease and every amendment, not from the seller's summary.",
